The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if ((u32)jiffies == list->last_gc)
    goto add_new_node;
if (collect > CONNCOUNT_GC_MAX_NODES)
    break;
Reason:
The first statement skips garbage collection (GC) entirely if the last GC occurred in the same jiffy. Combined with the second statement that limits the number of nodes cleaned per GC to 8 (CONNCOUNT_GC_MAX_NODES), the connection list can grow too fast when more than 8 new connections arrive in one jiffy. This causes the list->count to incorrectly reach the configured limit, leading to false positive connection limit violations.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if ((u32)jiffies == list->last_gc &&
    (list->count - list->last_gc_count) < CONNCOUNT_GC_MAX_COLLECT)
    goto add_new_node;
if (collect > CONNCOUNT_GC_MAX_COLLECT)
    break;
list->last_gc_count = list->count;
Reason:
The modified skip condition now only bypasses GC if the number of connections added since the last GC (list->count - list->last_gc_count) is below the higher collection limit of 64 (CONNCOUNT_GC_MAX_COLLECT). This prevents skipping GC when many new connections arrive in the same jiffy. The loop break limit is increased to 64 to allow more thorough cleanup per GC invocation. The new field list->last_gc_count is recorded after each GC to track the list size at that time, enabling the delta check.

